window.IM_DATA = {"title": "Insufficient Memory", "author": "Sean Fader", "stops": [{"index": 0, "id": "03487461251176E26D3C", "name": "Insufficient Memory", "kind": "intro", "html": "<p><i>Insufficient Memory</i> focuses on the years 1999/2000, just after Matthew Shepard’s brutal murder and President Clinton’s State of the Union address calling for legislation to respond to hate crimes committed against queer people (a bill would not be signed until 2009). </p><p>These years also promised progress in digital spaces with the advent of the Sony Digital Mavica, the first commercially successful camera that entered the market. After finding a Mavica camera in 2018, I drove 25,000 miles to 38 states where queer people were murdered in these same years, 1999 and 2000.</p><p>Using the digital technology of the time, the Mavica camera, I photographed and memorialized these sites. Since local queer newspapers were not digitized and local news outlets uninterested in these victims, many of their stories were difficult to research. There were often no narratives, only scraps of information in various newspapers or police blotters. </p><p>Upon returning home, I created this Google Earth Interactive Tour that can easily be accessed with the tiny URL <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://bit.ly/insufficentmemory\">http://bit.ly/insufficentmemory</a>. This is meant to allow anyone on the internet to travel across the country virtually, view the photographs I made from each site, and learn the stories of each of these murdered queer people.</p>", "n_images": 1, "photo": "photos/mavica_camera.jpg"}, {"index": 1, "id": "014C86E45A11756BCBF3", "name": "Lauryn Paige", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Austin, Texas, Murdered, January 8, 1999", "html": "<p>Austin, Texas, Murdered, January 8, 1999 </p><p>Eighteen-year-old Lauryn Paige was staying at her best friend Dixie’s house in Austin, Texas. That evening the two friends got dressed and went out to see some friends. Later that evening they headed to South Congress Avenue to make some money doing sex work. At five in the morning, they were tired and ready to go home.</p><p>They started walking south on Congress, looking for a ride back to Dixie’s neighborhood. A white conversion van kept circling and finally pulled over at the Circle K. In the van was Gamaliel Mireles Coria and his girlfriend’s brother, Frank Santos. Dixie and Lauryn asked for a ride home. Dixie later recalled saying to them, “Before we got into the van, the very first thing I told them was that we were transsexuals.” The four of them parked behind one of the car dealerships to party.</p><p>Lauryn and Gamaliel spent most of the time in the back of the van having sex. At about 6 a.m., Gamaliel dropped off Dixie and Frank, but Lauryn stayed with him.</p><p>The next morning, Lauryn’s body was found in a gutter in front of Tokyo Electron America in Austin. The murder was brutal. Authorities described it as “sadistic.” Lauryn sustained 14 blows to the head and at least 60 knife wounds.</p><p>Both police and news reports used male pronouns to refer to Lauryn and described her as a “man in a dress” or female impersonator.</p><p>There was no funeral service for Lauryn because her mother, Kathy, claimed, “We couldn’t have dressed him in a wig like a female, and it was the only way he would have wanted to be presented.”</p><p>Her father, Don, was quoted in the Austin American-Statesman shortly after Lauryn’s death, saying, “We just knew he wasn’t happy unless he dressed up....He’s been that way all of his life. We always knew he was different, and we pretty much accepted it, but we didn’t allow it around the house.” According to Lauryn’s friends, it was the family’s uneasiness that made her not want to spend much time at home. “She would tell me,” says Dixie, “‘I just don’t want to go home because I’ll have to hide who I am.’”</p><p>I made this photograph behind one of the car dealerships near where the four of them spent time partying in Gamaliel’s van.</p><p>The Lauryn Paige Fuller Story, from the Austin Chronicle:</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.austinchronicle.com/features/2000-02-18/75904/\">https://www.austinchronicle.com/features/2000-02-18/75904/</a></p>", "lon": -97.76150000000001, "lat": 30.2066667, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -97.7615, "lat": 30.2066667, "range": 898.2177492805953, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/lauryn_paige_mavica004.jpg", "still": "01_lauryn_paige_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 2, "id": "06529E202413D08E55A0", "name": "Lauryn Paige - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "N7pEbzhEHQqNmeS2cmdKRg", "camera": {"lon": -97.76141508465318, "lat": 30.20665972673831, "alt": 195.8003463049871, "heading": 169.4670282811521, "tilt": 86.07722455968532, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -97.76141508465318, "lat": 30.20665972673831, "for": "Lauryn Paige", "label": "Lauryn Paige - Street View", "still": "02_lauryn_paige_street.jpg"}, {"index": 3, "id": "023E0628341176DE10C8", "name": "Harold Jack Eisenhuth II", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Eldora, Colorado, Murdered, January 8, 1999", "html": "<p>Eldora, Colorado, Murdered, January 8, 1999 </p><p>Harold Jack Eisenhuth II had been a municipal judge in Broomfield, Lakeside, and other cities in Colorado. He also taught constitutional law at Metropolitan State College of Denver. He was found bludgeoned to death in the hallway of his Eldora home.</p><p>Matthew Joseph Garcia told police investigators that the two had gone for a drive and ended up at Harold’s Eldora home. Matthew said he was inside the home when Harold emerged nude from a bathroom and made advances toward him.</p><p>Matthew told authorities he hit Harold six times with a clawhammer because he thought he was going to sexually assault him.</p><p>The murder was not tried as a hate crime, and Matthew was only sentenced to five years in prison.</p><p>I made this photograph on the campus of Metropolitan State College of Denver, where Harold was a tenured faculty member.</p>", "lon": -105.0038889, "lat": 39.7422222, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -105.0038889, "lat": 39.7422222, "range": 789.9007075001945, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/harold_jack_eisenhuth_ii_mavica735.jpg", "still": "03_harold_jack_eisenhuth_ii_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 4, "id": "074E4CF8E113D0AA2030", "name": "Harold Jack Eisenhuth II - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "3MzDKvr84bY--tOpal3PZw", "camera": {"lon": -105.0041834024351, "lat": 39.74216615091655, "alt": 1586.993260040491, "heading": 120.4513634389938, "tilt": 94.08474032459283, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -105.0041834024351, "lat": 39.74216615091655, "for": "Harold Jack Eisenhuth II", "label": "Harold Jack Eisenhuth II - Street View", "still": "04_harold_jack_eisenhuth_ii_street.jpg"}, {"index": 5, "id": "0F35392A9013CF887648", "name": "Larry Morris and Kevin Tryals", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Texas City, Texas, Murdered, January 17, 1999", "html": "<p>Texas City, Texas, Murdered, January 17, 1999 </p><p>Larry Morris and Kevin Tryals were best friends. Larry was a hairdresser and had just purchased a brand-new Mustang the day before his death. Larry and Kevin spent the evening barhopping and driving around in Larry’s new car. Police responded to a report of heavy smoke near Loop 197 South. They found Larry’s car on fire as well as the two men. Both Larry and Kevin had been shot several times and set on fire: one inside the car and one on the pavement next to it. Both of them were wearing jewelry, so the police did not believe it was a burglary. An arrest was never made.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://www.texasobituaryproject.org/011799morris-tyals.html\">http://www.texasobituaryproject.org/011799morris-tyals.html</a></p><p>I made this photograph on the side of the road where they were discovered in Larry’s burnt-out Mustang.</p>", "lon": -94.92956670000001, "lat": 29.34007, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -94.9295667, "lat": 29.34007, "range": 906.8530554990739, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/laaron_morris_kevin_galvisto_mavica886.jpg", "still": "05_larry_morris_and_kevin_tryals_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 6, "id": "0ACCEB9E0C13D0ADC7D0", "name": "Larry Morris and Kevin Tryals - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "Vw-Ap27E3hGYVdZwEoZ_oA", "camera": {"lon": -94.92976162565886, "lat": 29.34016383572444, "alt": 2.366678419593835, "heading": 165.5919227982033, "tilt": 86.3447566784859, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -94.92976162565886, "lat": 29.34016383572444, "for": "Larry Morris and Kevin Tryals", "label": "Larry Morris and Kevin Tryals - Street View", "still": "06_larry_morris_and_kevin_tryals_street.jpg"}, {"index": 7, "id": "098A9C169B13CF9E5735", "name": "Robert Eads", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Toccoa, Georgia, Left to Die, January 17, 1999", "html": "<p>Toccoa, Georgia, Left to Die, January 17, 1999 </p><p>Robert Eads was a trans man from Toccoa, Georgia. He had married a man and had two children, then came out as a lesbian before finally transitioning. Though he wanted bottom surgery, doctors at the time suggested he not do it due to his age. In 1996 he was rushed to the hospital for abdominal pains, and it was discovered that he had ovarian cancer. More than a dozen doctors refused to treat him, afraid that word would get out and their business would be negatively impacted. By the time Robert was finally able to get treatment, the cancer had metastasized and had spread to several other organs. He died in a nursing home.</p><p>I was unable to find the location of Robert's home. This photograph was taken in the hilly town of Toccoa where he lived.</p><p>The documentary <i>Southern Comfort</i> tells his story: <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IH0L3wlV0hg\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IH0L3wlV0hg</a></p>", "lon": -83.34689499999999, "lat": 34.550555, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -83.34689499999999, "lat": 34.550555, "range": 851.7961672273369, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/robert_eads_mavica209.jpg", "still": "07_robert_eads_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 8, "id": "04D603DAD913D0B06405", "name": "Robert Eads - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "5JQZfGUrcW1U1JD1DSQzNg", "camera": {"lon": -83.34689582275124, "lat": 34.5505987350462, "alt": 265.7726242182522, "heading": 219.8412746029929, "tilt": 102.7091110670144, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -83.34689582275124, "lat": 34.5505987350462, "for": "Robert Eads", "label": "Robert Eads - Street View", "still": "08_robert_eads_street.jpg"}, {"index": 9, "id": "05F7BD2D8D13D4C3966E", "name": "Robert Eads", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "This is the location of Sun Alliance Hospice, where Robert Eads died.", "html": "<p>This is the location of Sun Alliance Hospice, where Robert Eads died.</p>", "lon": -84.29984033835109, "lat": 33.93011839068296, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -84.29984033835109, "lat": 33.93011839068296, "range": 850, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)", "photo": "photos/robert_eads_2_mavica248.jpg"}, {"index": 10, "id": "0310D990CD13D4C565D6", "name": "Robert Eads", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "oS0XsQjSECyr_8856PyarQ", "camera": {"lon": -84.29955266841209, "lat": 33.92975929590384, "alt": 313.6793604279089, "heading": 322.0106895407615, "tilt": 71.30892356562113, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -84.29955266841209, "lat": 33.92975929590384, "for": "Robert Eads", "label": "Robert Eads - Street View", "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 11, "id": "02B1D39CEB13CFA232A5", "name": "Steve Dwayne Garcia", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Houston, Texas, Murdered, February 6, 1999", "html": "<p>Houston, Texas, Murdered, February 6, 1999 </p><p>Montrose residents returning home from a party found Steve Dwayne Garcia dead on the sidewalk. The reports say that he was dressed in women’s clothing and had on makeup. It’s unclear from reports how Steve thought about his gender or if he used his birth name. Steve had been shot in the back but was still wearing jewelry, possibly eliminating the theory that his death was part of a robbery. At first police investigated his death as a hate crime, but later they reversed course, saying the evidence showed it was not motivated by hate. The police refused to share the evidence.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://www.texasobituaryproject.org/020699garcia.html\">http://www.texasobituaryproject.org/020699garcia.html</a></p><p>I made this photograph on the side of the road approximately where Steve's body was found.</p>", "lon": -95.3885033, "lat": 29.75482, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -95.3885033, "lat": 29.75482, "range": 902.7469351121379, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/steve_dwayne_garcia_mavica147.jpg", "still": "09_steve_dwayne_garcia_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 12, "id": "0F0960BB1013D0B1DEF5", "name": "Steve Dwayne Garcia - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "qC7qMNWhUStXksW_5M6JkA", "camera": {"lon": -95.38846167735375, "lat": 29.75482263573647, "alt": 18.56484031619596, "heading": 169.6816020191885, "tilt": 88.66733943294192, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -95.38846167735375, "lat": 29.75482263573647, "for": "Steve Dwayne Garcia", "label": "Steve Dwayne Garcia - Street View", "still": "10_steve_dwayne_garcia_street.jpg"}, {"index": 13, "id": "0C7EFBEC3413CA24A85F", "name": "Adam Colton", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Novato, California, Beaten, February 12, 1999", "html": "<p>Novato, California, Beaten, February 12, 1999 </p><p>When Adam Colton was 17, he transferred to San Marin High School. An intelligent, highly articulate, and out teenager, he told the principal that he wanted to help form the Gay–Straight Alliance at school. “I’ve decided that I’d really like to be an activist and do something to help make changes at San Marin,\" he said. Adam was verbally open about his sexuality, wore gay-positive T-shirts and, on occasion, eyeshadow. In September, a week after talking with his principal, he found gay slurs smeared in ketchup on his car in the school parking lot, and later that night he was jumped by three unidentified young men outside a downtown supermarket in Novato. Two weeks later, someone wrote anti-gay slurs in lighter fluid on his family’s driveway and tried to ignite them.</p><p>On February 12, Adam was attacked and wandered semiconscious into a classroom, where he passed out. He later awoke in the hospital with no memory of the attack or where it took place. His attackers used a pen to scratch the word “fag” into his arm and stomach. City officials in Novato called the attack a hate crime.</p><p>I made this photograph on the idyllic high school campus where Adam was assaulted.</p>", "lon": -122.6095956088305, "lat": 38.1192323719516, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -122.6094031895557, "lat": 38.11911312894573, "range": 239.1276731610197, "heading": 156.7112197130909, "tilt": 44.99637031775681}, "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)", "photo": "photos/adam_colton_mavica017.jpg"}, {"index": 14, "id": "065E257F2013D0B7BF24", "name": "Adam Colton - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "vmG4erHaIgwgeI9sTcYkUw", "camera": {"lon": -122.6090932070671, "lat": 38.11898846077365, "alt": 28.9129876196533, "heading": 291.0769543045055, "tilt": 88.85135432365404, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -122.6090932070671, "lat": 38.11898846077365, "for": "Adam Colton", "label": "Adam Colton - Street View", "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 15, "id": "09E018648113CB21AAB1", "name": "Billy Jack Gaither", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Sylacauga, Alabama, Murdered, February 19, 1999", "html": "<p>Sylacauga, Alabama, Murdered, February 19, 1999 </p><p>Billy Jack Gaither was a 39-year-old gay man who lived with his disabled parents in Sylacauga, Alabama. He cared for them and worked at the nearby Russell Athletics apparel company. His body was found burned on top of a pile of tires on the side of a creek outside of town.</p><p>His throat was cut, and his body was savagely beaten before being set on fire. The two men who carried out the murder, Steven Mullins and Charles Monroe Butler, claimed that Billy Jack had come on to them and his sexuality was why they killed him. Witnesses came forward and testified that Steven had had sex with men before and that he may have had a relationship with Billy Jack prior to killing him. In June 1999 Steven Mullins pled guilty to capital murder; Charles Butler stood trial and was found guilty of the same charge by a jury. In August 1999 both men were sentenced to life in prison without parole. In March 2019 Steven was fatally stabbed in prison.</p><p>The Billy Jack Gaither Humanitarian Award recognizes “an individual or organization that has shown extraordinary courage in the struggle against hatred and contributed to the creation of a just society.”</p><p>The location where Billy Jack was murdered was difficult to find. It's deep in the Talladega National Forest. I read Steven's confession over and over and jotted down his description of a small sign with a boat on it marking the gravel road into the forest. When I arrived in Sylacauga, I drove out on Millerville Highway, looking for a small sign with a boat on it. My phone overheated and shut off. After holding it over the AC vent for a while, the phone turned on, and I realized I had gone way beyond where I thought I should have. I looked for a place to turn around. I pulled into a gravel road to make a U-turn, looked up, and there was the small sign with the boat. I would have never seen it driving by. I drove into the forest, turning left and then right as Steven described in his confessional. When I arrived at the boat launch, the sky opened up and it began to pour.</p><p>“The Life and Death of Billy Jack Gaither,” from the series Frontline, broadcast on PBS: <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/assault/billyjack/\">https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/assault/billyjack/</a></p><p>Here is the link to Steven’s confession.  I used Steven’s directions to find the boat slip.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/assault/billyjack/statement.html\">https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/assault/billyjack/statement.html</a></p>", "lon": -86.1735467, "lat": 33.1862917, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -86.1735467, "lat": 33.1862917, "range": 866.9257752459572, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/billy_jack_gaither_mavica118.jpg", "still": "11_billy_jack_gaither_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 16, "id": "036DBA03B91DDB1DC0DF", "name": "Billy Jack Gaither - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "ZTnD_GupQSzUxJ6AI2mS6Q", "camera": {"lon": -86.14905282084578, "lat": 33.17697737219477, "alt": 262.3785705566406, "heading": 353.1890493040954, "tilt": 82.84685040874939, "fov": 32.15821696030265}, "lon": -86.14905282084578, "lat": 33.17697737219477, "for": "Billy Jack Gaither", "label": "Billy Jack Gaither - Street View", "still": "12_billy_jack_gaither_street.jpg"}, {"index": 17, "id": "05458B42E413CB1DF404", "name": "Billy Jack Gaither", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "", "html": "<p>This is the location of the Toolbox, Billy Jack Gaither’s favorite gay bar in Birmingham. The Toolbox eventually burned down.</p>", "lon": -86.7564617, "lat": 33.5325133, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -86.7564617, "lat": 33.5325133, "range": 863.1329001699529, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)", "photo": "photos/billy_jack_gaither_mavica122.jpg"}, {"index": 18, "id": "0C72AFF31513D0B96E53", "name": "Billy Jack Gaither", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "D0xPvYXCe9eP7nj8eXpCaA", "camera": {"lon": -86.75649804796336, "lat": 33.53240262158701, "alt": 205.5629272460937, "heading": 322.8321407561032, "tilt": 90.41429004785284, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -86.75649804796336, "lat": 33.53240262158701, "for": "Billy Jack Gaither", "label": "Billy Jack Gaither - Street View", "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 19, "id": "038828D8D813CB2EFCCC", "name": "Eddie Matthews", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Detroit, Michigan, Murdered, February 20, 1999", "html": "<p>Detroit, Michigan, Murdered, February 20, 1999 </p><p>Eddie Matthews was a Detroit nursing home employee who was found in his home strangled by a telephone cord and a neck scarf. Money and other items were missing from his apartment. The police struggled to find a suspect. However, Eddie’s murder was almost identical to Harold McCormick’s murder, which would happen several months later. The police matched Willie Brown’s DNA at Eddie’s home and believed that Willie was targeting gay black men in the cruising area of Palmer Park.</p><p>Some trans day of remembrance sites identify Eddie as Chris Muzette (I believe). I could not find any information about what name or pronoun Eddie preferred, so I continue to use male pronouns.</p><p>I made this photograph of the lot where his house used to stand. </p>", "lon": -83.0651639, "lat": 42.37412, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -83.0651639, "lat": 42.37412, "range": 756.0861124472932, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/eddie_matthews_mavica434.jpg", "still": "13_eddie_matthews_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 20, "id": "0B1FC1D48913D0BB5F96", "name": "Eddie Matthews - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "TwU9tvvY7hNxOgv_JrEX1Q", "camera": {"lon": -83.065257688331, "lat": 42.37425558192675, "alt": 193.3065811418803, "heading": 194.5436888456284, "tilt": 88.97280790059959, "fov": 59.99999355724658}, "lon": -83.065257688331, "lat": 42.37425558192675, "for": "Eddie Matthews", "label": "Eddie Matthews - Street View", "still": "14_eddie_matthews_street.jpg"}, {"index": 21, "id": "02B409BFB813CFB2E584", "name": "Unknown Person", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Houston, Texas, Murdered, February 25, 1999", "html": "<p>Houston, Texas, Murdered, February 25, 1999 </p><p>I’m not sure how to identify this person since the few articles on them reported on “an unknown male prostitute” or a “cross-dressing man.” He or she was found shot to death in a hotel room. I could not find any information on what gender they might have preferred or why they were assumed to be a prostitute. They were the second queer person murdered in a three-week period in Houston in 1999.</p><p>I made this photograph where the hotel used to stand. Now it’s the location of a luxury apartment complex.</p>", "lon": -95.38536999999998, "lat": 29.69953, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -95.38537, "lat": 29.69953, "range": 903.2971590220948, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/unknown_person_mavica392.jpg", "still": "15_unknown_person_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 22, "id": "0D5F284F3513D0BC74DA", "name": "Unknown Person - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "1U66ZHFVMVhvA5RzE2Zs-w", "camera": {"lon": -95.38545558726258, "lat": 29.69955782848041, "alt": 15.80733358280294, "heading": 125.6053267434991, "tilt": 84.29963647845088, "fov": 59.99999999098642}, "lon": -95.38545558726258, "lat": 29.69955782848041, "for": "Unknown Person", "label": "Unknown Person - Street View", "still": "16_unknown_person_street.jpg"}, {"index": 23, "id": "0D1B0144881176D5FA6F", "name": "Tracey Thompson", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Seville, Georgia, Murdered, March 30, 1999", "html": "<p>Seville, Georgia, Murdered, March 30, 1999 </p><p>Tracey Thompson, a 33-year-old trans woman, was attacked with a bat in rural Georgia on March 30, 1999. She stumbled half a mile along a dirt road and up to a house in Seville, Georgia. Bleeding from the head, she asked for water, a blanket, and a place to rest. Joel and Cindy Brown took her in and called 911. Tracey was taken to a local hospital where she ultimately died.</p><p>On the dirt road about a half-mile from the farmhouse, police found blood and shattered pieces of a baseball bat. On her deathbed, Tracey told authorities she’d been struck repeatedly with a baseball bat by an ex-boyfriend. Police tracked him down. He claimed he was out of town at the time, and the police didn’t question him again. No one has ever been arrested for the crime.</p><p>News outlets used male pronouns and described her as a \"man in a dress.\" The local news even quoted area retiree Fred King saying, “They say he was wearing a dress. Most people in this area would say, ‘Hell, he needed killing.’”</p><p>TG Rage, a San Francisco transgender rights group, declared a national state of emergency after Tracey was killed, citing the fact that trans women had the highest murder rate in the country. Trans women, especially of color, are being murdered at extremely high rates to this day.</p><p>The dirt road reveals the red Georgia soil. When I came upon the Browns’ house, my heart sunk. They had been so kind to her. I wondered if they still lived there. I made this photograph approximately half a mile down the dirt road from the Browns’ farmhouse.</p>", "lon": -83.6083889, "lat": 31.97186109999999, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -83.6083889, "lat": 31.9718611, "range": 879.974558874678, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/tracey_thompson_mavica501.jpg", "still": "17_tracey_thompson_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 24, "id": "0484F0209613D0BE4A83", "name": "Tracey Thompson - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "adurVGDWfNhW80ai8mZGJw", "camera": {"lon": -83.60848102960468, "lat": 31.97182598181955, "alt": 113.6419448852539, "heading": 89.82883255063096, "tilt": 96.48733193474304, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -83.60848102960468, "lat": 31.97182598181955, "for": "Tracey Thompson", "label": "Tracey Thompson - Street View", "still": "18_tracey_thompson_street.jpg"}, {"index": 25, "id": "0B8EF8FE9F13D4DAB53F", "name": "Harold McCormick", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Detroit, Michigan, Murdered, May 8, 1999", "html": "<p>Detroit, Michigan, Murdered, May 8, 1999 </p><p>Harold McCormick was a social worker and civil rights activist, and he served in the United States Army for six years.</p><p>Harold met Willie Brown in the cruising area of Palmer Park, and the two went to the Uptown Bookstore in Highland Park and rented videos. They went back to Harold’s apartment, where Willie strangled Harold with a tie and a telephone cord, stole jewelry and clothing, and left with a rented 1999 Grand Am.</p><p>Police found CCTV footage of the two men renting movies and were able to identify Willie, a paroled murderer and prostitute, and tracked him down. They were able to tie him to the killing of Eddie Matthews because the murders were so similar.</p><p>I made this photograph in front of his home outside Detroit.  This stone felt like a marker to me. </p>", "lon": -83.19790499999999, "lat": 42.4782967, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -83.19790499999999, "lat": 42.4782967, "range": 754.7161052100154, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/harold_mccormick_mavica491.jpg", "still": "19_harold_mccormick_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 26, "id": "00F196F93F13D4DFF67E", "name": "Harold McCormick - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "Oq1qC-4X-86tRR97oNEJ8Q", "camera": {"lon": -83.20188573031476, "lat": 42.47836508349997, "alt": 208.6752777103518, "heading": 87.0671653848128, "tilt": 91.33868420329257, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -83.20188573031476, "lat": 42.47836508349997, "for": "Harold McCormick", "label": "Harold McCormick - Street View", "still": "20_harold_mccormick_street.jpg"}, {"index": 27, "id": "04D82A0E4E13D67C7B83", "name": "Uptown Bookstore", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "", "html": "<p>Police were able to tie the murders of Eddie Matthews and Harold McCormick to the same killer. Both men were seen, along with their killer, on video footage from the Uptown Bookstore.</p>", "lon": -83.1050739087666, "lat": 42.41745466795247, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -83.10513372668389, "lat": 42.41743315033634, "range": 500.0252310070937, "heading": 228.497862075886, "tilt": 0}, "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)", "photo": "photos/eddie_matthews_harold_mccormick_mavica452.jpg"}, {"index": 28, "id": "06BD97E50413D4D2203A", "name": "Uptown Bookstore", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "rjG0IIKlamP1AmSHtiFaow", "camera": {"lon": -83.10513372665913, "lat": 42.41743315042424, "alt": 196.9955988994372, "heading": 228.497862075886, "tilt": 85.23342882331015, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -83.10513372665913, "lat": 42.41743315042424, "for": "Uptown Bookstore", "label": "Uptown Bookstore - Street View", "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 29, "id": "0B44D2059313CF94B11F", "name": "Michael Fleming", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Baton Rouge, Louisiana, Murdered, June 1, 1999", "html": "<p>Baton Rouge, Louisiana, Murdered, June 1, 1999 </p><p>Michael Fleming was targeted on the street in his neighborhood by a group of men who lived in the area. According to reports, one of them said they “had a problem with him.” The men fought with Fleming in the street, beating him with some kind of weapon, and stomping him until he was unconscious. He died of severe head injuries. Quincy Powell was arrested, and during his interview with police, he referred to Michael as “faggot Mike.” Quincy was convicted of second-degree murder, his defense attorney claiming the prosecution failed to prove that Fleming’s sexual orientation was a factor in the killing.</p><p>I made this photograph of the ditch alongside the road, approximately where they beat him to death. The ditch was filled with garbage.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://congress.gov/crec/2004/05/19/modified/CREC-2004-05-19-pt1-PgS5794.htm\">https://congress.gov/crec/2004/05/19/modified/CREC-2004-05-19-pt1-PgS5794.htm</a></p>", "lon": -91.13175279999999, "lat": 30.4967533, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -91.1317528, "lat": 30.49675329999999, "range": 895.279530748715, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/michael_flemming_mavica181.jpg", "still": "21_michael_fleming_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 30, "id": "08193DFBF413D4E5AF1C", "name": "Michael Fleming - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "FCDfVJpQDEQnKpMvPfvZcQ", "camera": {"lon": -91.13155768996889, "lat": 30.49647255608444, "alt": 20.99071693316629, "heading": 176.3859798492421, "tilt": 84.88173456847147, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -91.13155768996889, "lat": 30.49647255608444, "for": "Michael Fleming", "label": "Michael Fleming - Street View", "still": "22_michael_fleming_street.jpg"}, {"index": 31, "id": "0D471EB96E13CB42F9AD", "name": "Dennis Dolinger", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Washington, DC, Murdered, June 4, 1999", "html": "<p>Washington, DC, Murdered, June 4, 1999 </p><p>Dennis Dolinger was an advisory neighborhood commissioner and the founder of his Capitol Hill neighborhood crime-fighting group. He was found dead in his home, stabbed 25 times in the head and neck with a Phillips-head screwdriver. His house had been torn apart, and jewelry and nearly $1,000 in cash were missing. DC police found blood from Dennis and a second man splattered all over Dennis’s home. They ran the second set of DNA through the FBI database and were able to identify Raymond Anthony Jenkins. Though Raymond was subsequently found guilty, the use of the FBI database proved to be a legal problem for over a decade.</p><p>Both Dennis and Raymond liked to hang out in Dupont Circle at a gay bar called the Fireplace. The day after Dennis’s murder, Raymond showed up with scratches on his face, hands, and arm and carrying a large amount of cash, a diamond ring, and a bunch of little gold chains.</p><p>Shortly after Dennis’s death, friends and neighbors persuaded the DC Council to name a small triangular park located across the street from his house the Dennis Dolinger Memorial Park. A memorial stone with a plaque with Dolinger’s name and an inscription honoring him have been placed in the park.</p><p>He is the only person in the group of queer people documented in this project that has a memorial erected in their memory.</p><p>The <i>Washington Blade</i> does a great job on this story:</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.washingtonblade.com/2019/05/02/new-information-surfaces-in-1999-d-c-gay-murder/\">https://www.washingtonblade.com/2019/05/02/new-information-surfaces-in-1999-d-c-gay-murder/</a></p>", "lon": -76.9826367, "lat": 38.8813783, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -76.9826367, "lat": 38.88137830000001, "range": 800.6174218240807, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/dennis_dolinger_mavica557.jpg", "still": "23_dennis_dolinger_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 32, "id": "0F103FD8D213D4F3804D", "name": "Dennis Dolinger - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "gRdOlyk7RWnZVZrXHPu7IA", "camera": {"lon": -76.98284521659224, "lat": 38.88145222205998, "alt": 22.3339786529541, "heading": 108.6937029480437, "tilt": 95.92502252382377, "fov": 59.99999999582647}, "lon": -76.98284521659224, "lat": 38.88145222205998, "for": "Dennis Dolinger", "label": "Dennis Dolinger - Street View", "still": "24_dennis_dolinger_street.jpg"}, {"index": 33, "id": "0EDA04B86213CB46C632", "name": "Emmon Bodfish", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Orinda, California, Murdered, late June 1999", "html": "<p>Orinda, California, Murdered, late June 1999 </p><p>Emmon Bodfish led a double life. As Emmon, he lived in a simple home on seven acres in a wealthy suburb of San Francisco and ran a pool maintenance and plumbing business; as Margaret Bodfish, she lived in a townhouse in Marin. Some people thought Emmon was Margaret’s son. Emmon was part of the Reformed Druids of North America and produced the <i>Druid Missal-Any Magazine</i> from 1983 to 1991. Emmon wrote extensively in his journal about hating his body and wanting to be beaten to death. Police found his body bludgeoned to death at his home in Orinda. There was no sign of robbery, but they did find a hidden safe in the floor containing $60,000 and a drill close to it. The drill had not been used. Hours after finding out about his mother’s death, Emmon’s son committed suicide in a hotel in Santa Monica. He left behind a suicide note that read: </p><p>“I have know (sic) that if I were to die before my mother the effect on her would be devastating. She has my predisposition to melancholy (this choice of word is too weak) and the additional crosses to bear of chronic insomnia, and headache probably much worse than mine. I was the closest person to her in her life; sadly, she had few friends to turn to for comfort in her dark times.”</p><p>Several police officers who had searched Emmon’s house for clues contracted Q Fever, a rare and serious disease.</p><p>The John Muir Land Trust took ownership of the property to create the Bodfish Preserve, a public park with woods and trails. Its website refers to Emmon as Margaret and claims the house is easy to visit, but it is impossible to get to unless you live next to it. </p><p>This link contains some of the information about his writing in <i>Druid Missal-Any Magazine</i>:</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://zagria.blogspot.com/2013/08/emmon-bodfish-1943-1999-druid-plumber.html#.Xs6y6RNKjUI\">https://zagria.blogspot.com/2013/08/emmon-bodfish-1943-1999-druid-plumber.html#.Xs6y6RNKjUI</a></p>", "lon": -122.5338283, "lat": 37.86952, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -122.5338283, "lat": 37.86952, "range": 812.9894473004831, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/emmon_bodfish_mavica611.jpg", "still": "25_emmon_bodfish_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 34, "id": "050AED3E2F13D4F5093D", "name": "Emmon Bodfish - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "bQpp3P-23Wxr9eEvYJvGTg", "camera": {"lon": -122.5337833659678, "lat": 37.86974709961731, "alt": 130.2365743778343, "heading": 182.0863552702933, "tilt": 92.62879224183652, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -122.5337833659678, "lat": 37.86974709961731, "for": "Emmon Bodfish", "label": "Emmon Bodfish - Street View", "still": "26_emmon_bodfish_street.jpg"}, {"index": 35, "id": "04B33C402813D4EA3792", "name": "John Carol Lloyd", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Gainesville, Florida, Murdered, July 1, 1999", "html": "<p>Gainesville, Florida, Murdered, July 1, 1999 </p><p>I was never able to find any information about John’s death except that it was mentioned in a list of murdered queer people. I did find out that he owned an insurance agency. This is where he lived at the time of his death.</p>", "lon": -82.5485217, "lat": 29.7274117, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -82.5485217, "lat": 29.7274117, "range": 903.0198004962607, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/john_carol_lloyd_mvc-015s.jpg", "still": "27_john_carol_lloyd_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 36, "id": "003ADDFE3D13D4F24834", "name": "John Carol Lloyd - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "0sq6Jh7w7qA90yiOg_1TkA", "camera": {"lon": -82.54837936370548, "lat": 29.72737947486406, "alt": 28.01464652999068, "heading": 19.37047453554605, "tilt": 91.5000439226283, "fov": 59.99999999999984}, "lon": -82.54837936370548, "lat": 29.72737947486406, "for": "John Carol Lloyd", "label": "John Carol Lloyd - Street View", "still": "28_john_carol_lloyd_street.jpg"}, {"index": 37, "id": "0813B80D351176D8F10C", "name": "Gary Matson and Winfield Mowder", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Happy Valley, California, Murdered July 1, 1999", "html": "<p>Happy Valley, California, Murdered July 1, 1999 </p><p>Gary Matson and Winfield Mowder were together for 14 years and lived in Happy Valley, California, a rural community a few hours outside of San Francisco. Gary was a horticulturist and helped found several local institutions: the Mount Shasta Farmers Market, the Redding Arboretum, and the Carter House, a science museum for kids. Winfield had just completed his bachelor’s degree in anthropology at California State University Chico.</p><p>Benjamin Matthew Williams and James Tyler Williams were brothers who operated a landscaping and lawn service out of their parents’ home in Palo Cedro, California. They knew Gary and Winfield from the farmers market.</p><p>The brothers, who went by their middle names, broke into Gary and Winfield’s home in the middle of the night. Matthew and Tyler forced the two men to make a new recording on their answering machine saying they had left town. They then forced Gary and Winfield into their platform bed, which stood seven feet off the floor. The killers stood on chairs at the foot of the bed and fired at the couple. Gary received five shots to the head and one to the back. Winfield was shot seven times in the head and once in the neck.</p><p>The couple was found naked in their bed with shell casings from a .22 caliber gun littering the floor. The walls and ceiling were stained with blood. There was no sign of forced entry and no signs of robbery.</p><p>Matthew and Tyler decided to kill Gary and Winfield because they were gay. The brothers had both become radicalized by white supremacist groups they had connected with online. Later, in his account of the murders, Tyler said that his brother did not consider the killing of Gary and Winfield to be murder, but a “judgment.”</p><p>Matthew and Tyler also confessed to the June 18, 1999, arson attacks against three synagogues in Sacramento—Congregation B'nai Israel, Congregation Beth Shalom, and Kenesset Israel Torah Center.</p><p>Matthew committed suicide in jail days before his sentencing. Tyler pleaded guilty to the murders. Under a plea agreement, Tyler was sentenced to 29 years to life, to be served after a 21-year sentence for the synagogue arsons. If he had gone to trial, Tyler could have received the death penalty.</p><p>I made this photograph looking at the sky above the 100-year-old olive trees in front of Gary and Winfield’s home in Happy Valley.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Gary_Matson_and_Winfield_Mowder\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Gary_Matson_and_Winfield_Mowder</a></p>", "lon": -122.4357778, "lat": 40.4485833, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -122.4357778, "lat": 40.4485833, "range": 780.9786471672007, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/gary_matson_and_winfield_mowder_mavica647.jpg", "still": "29_gary_matson_and_winfield_mowder_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 38, "id": "05377BC0C413D4F70C94", "name": "Gary Matson and Winfield Mowder - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "ITolRzkuIh42kljQ09TATQ", "camera": {"lon": -122.4356974964674, "lat": 40.44859715414388, "alt": 251.0939107197013, "heading": 273.5317853546169, "tilt": 101.3027465763824, "fov": 59.99986027319857}, "lon": -122.4356974964674, "lat": 40.44859715414388, "for": "Gary Matson and Winfield Mowder", "label": "Gary Matson and Winfield Mowder - Street View", "still": "30_gary_matson_and_winfield_mowder_street.jpg"}, {"index": 39, "id": "0CCF352C7313CF982159", "name": "PFC Barry L. Winchell", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Fort Campbell, Kentucky, Murdered, July 5, 1999", "html": "<p>Fort Campbell, Kentucky, Murdered, July 5, 1999 </p><p>Barry L. Winchell was an infantry soldier in the United States Army, whose murder by a fellow soldier, Calvin Glover, became a point of reference in the ongoing debate about the policy known as “Don't Ask, Don't Tell,” which did not allow gays, bisexuals, and lesbians serving in the US military to be open about their sexual orientation.</p><p>Barry’s roommate, Justin Fisher, took him to The Connection, a Nashville Club that featured trans performers, where he met Calpernia Addams. Barry and Calpernia began dating, and when word got out about their relationship, Barry started getting harassed on the base. Tensions grew between Calvin, Justin, and Barry, coming to a fatal end when Calvin beat Barry to death with a baseball bat while Barry was sleeping.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2000/05/kentucky-murder-200005\">https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2000/05/kentucky-murder-200005</a></p>", "lon": -87.4367367, "lat": 36.644505, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -87.4367367, "lat": 36.644505, "range": 827.6348022984887, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/pvt__barry_winchell_mvc-007s.jpg", "still": "31_pfc_barry_l_winchell_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 40, "id": "01156F444113D5005EF8", "name": "PFC Barry L. Winchell - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "6JjtfIBa3dwzH0za_JgJ8w", "camera": {"lon": -87.43687145941203, "lat": 36.6446852420384, "alt": 169.5515747070312, "heading": 286.3354341887328, "tilt": 98.11159783251792, "fov": 59.99999999999866}, "lon": -87.43687145941203, "lat": 36.6446852420384, "for": "PFC Barry L. Winchell", "label": "PFC Barry L. Winchell - Street View", "still": "32_pfc_barry_l_winchell_street.jpg"}, {"index": 41, "id": "072E2F542A13D4FD84AB", "name": "PFC Barry L. Winchell", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "This is the site of The Connection, a Nashville Club where Barry met Calpernia.", "html": "<p>This is the site of The Connection, a Nashville Club where Barry met Calpernia.</p>", "lon": -86.7810367, "lat": 36.1894533, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -86.7810367, "lat": 36.1894533, "range": 832.9802441800754, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 42, "id": "0D1F9DA17813D501DDAB", "name": "PFC Barry L. Winchell", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "Tzq9nye3S1CgrGzQFqG7Sw", "camera": {"lon": -86.78094803883324, "lat": 36.18950570156825, "alt": 125.7848055520672, "heading": 62.18696510585676, "tilt": 89.21931274783172, "fov": 59.99999977544559}, "lon": -86.78094803883324, "lat": 36.18950570156825, "for": "PFC Barry L. Winchell", "label": "PFC Barry L. Winchell - Street View", "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 43, "id": "0CD6A9FA1313CB0DE379", "name": "Baretta Williams", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Chicago, Illinois, Murdered, July 28, 1999", "html": "<p>Chicago, Illinois, Murdered, July 28, 1999 </p><p>                         </p><p>After Pebbles and Baretta Williams arrived home from a night at the Baton Club, Baretta announced that her new boyfriend was coming over. His name was Michael Key, and he arrived with three friends. Baretta had been saving for gender-affirmation surgery, and Michael wanted to steal her money. So he shot Baretta once at point-blank range, killing her.</p><p>Michael approached Pebbles and shot her once in the back of the head, once in the face, and once in the arm. The last thing Pebbles remembered was the ambulance ride to Northwestern Memorial Hospital, where she awakened several days later to discover that Baretta was dead.</p><p>The police did not release many details of the crime for quite a while, and I was unable to find the address of Baretta’s home where the crime took place. The police claimed they were attempting to protect Pebbles. The only information I was able to find was that Baretta’s home was on the South Side. I plugged “South Side Chicago” into my Google Maps app and drove there. This is that location.</p><p>The <i>Windy City Times</i> did a great job with this story:</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Shooting-victim-remembers/33071.html\">http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Shooting-victim-remembers/33071.html</a></p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Left-For-Dead-Pebbles-Finds-Courage-to-Testify/1649.html\">http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Left-For-Dead-Pebbles-Finds-Courage-to-Testify/1649.html</a></p>", "lon": -87.6088467, "lat": 41.804245, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -87.6088467, "lat": 41.804245, "range": 763.538699689245, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/baretta_williams_mavica187.jpg", "still": "33_baretta_williams_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 44, "id": "0B360BD6F413D507D1CA", "name": "Baretta Williams - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "ToS9zAHw3HgBMiiDJdumhw", "camera": {"lon": -87.60888033528727, "lat": 41.80418026188739, "alt": 183.1049652101126, "heading": 182.938675873203, "tilt": 97.28707644470279, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -87.60888033528727, "lat": 41.80418026188739, "for": "Baretta Williams", "label": "Baretta Williams - Street View", "still": "34_baretta_williams_street.jpg"}, {"index": 45, "id": "07EB6002F0117575BC58", "name": "Pebbles", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Chicago, Illinois, Shot Multiple Times, July 28, 1999", "html": "<p>Chicago, Illinois, Shot Multiple Times, July 28, 1999 </p><p>After Pebbles and Baretta Williams arrived home from a night at the Baton Club, Baretta announced that her new boyfriend was coming over. His name was Michael Key, and he arrived with three friends. Baretta had been saving for gender-affirmation surgery, and Michael wanted to steal her money. So he shot Baretta once at point-blank range, killing her.</p><p>Michael approached Pebbles and shot her once in the back of the head, once in the face, and once in the arm. The last thing Pebbles remembered was the ambulance ride to Northwestern Memorial Hospital, where she awakened several days later to discover that Baretta was dead.</p><p>I shot this image outside of Northwestern Memorial Hospital’s Emergency Room Entrance.</p><p>The <i>Windy City Times</i> did a great job with this story:</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Shooting-victim-remembers/33071.html\">http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Shooting-victim-remembers/33071.html</a></p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Left-For-Dead-Pebbles-Finds-Courage-to-Testify/1649.html\">http://www.windycitymediagroup.com/lgbt/Left-For-Dead-Pebbles-Finds-Courage-to-Testify/1649.html</a></p>", "lon": -87.6218056, "lat": 41.8943056, "n_images": 2, "lookat": {"lon": -87.6218056, "lat": 41.8943056, "range": 762.3656440443485, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)", "photo": "photos/pebbles_mavica182.jpg"}, {"index": 46, "id": "0B0316E63313D508A4A0", "name": "Pebbles - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "WJT2ao9bgQWl4KN-W--4Fg", "camera": {"lon": -87.62176077218257, "lat": 41.89420130392252, "alt": 183.2534722447112, "heading": 40.06248820871588, "tilt": 90.92666109678377, "fov": 59.9999394225662}, "lon": -87.62176077218257, "lat": 41.89420130392252, "for": "Pebbles", "label": "Pebbles - Street View", "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 47, "id": "045B9A104813D5066E4D", "name": "Baton Club", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "", "html": "<p dir=\"ltr\">Pebbles and Baretta spent the evening at the Baton Club before returning home to the South Side.<br></p>", "lon": -87.6587133, "lat": 41.967545, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -87.6587133, "lat": 41.967545, "range": 761.4103765635067, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 48, "id": "06A78D912313D5099838", "name": "Baton Club", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "5zX78DqvKdNhgd27Gxz4qw", "camera": {"lon": -87.65878787257087, "lat": 41.96751305196346, "alt": 182.2360468956779, "heading": 62.29438246007645, "tilt": 93.79479202608536, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -87.65878787257087, "lat": 41.96751305196346, "for": "Baton Club", "label": "Baton Club - Street View", "hidden": true, "hidden_reason": "removed from the live project (Sept 2026)"}, {"index": 49, "id": "0DC109A5F413CAECACD9", "name": "Alfred Moon", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Providence, Rhode Island, Murdered, August 19, 1999", "html": "<p>Providence, Rhode Island, Murdered, August 19, 1999 </p><p>Dr. Alfred Moon was a divorced father of three who worked as a radiologist at Rhode Island Hospital and taught at Brown University. Dedicated to his job, he brought CAT scans to Rhode Island for the first time. On August 21, Alfred’s housekeeper found Moon’s beaten, naked body in bed, with blood splattered as high as the ceiling. His face was so badly disfigured that police needed dental records and fingerprints to identify him.</p><p>The case remained cold for five years until the police received an anonymous letter that Angel Navarro had murdered Alfred. Angel eventually pleaded guilty. In his confession, he said he “had two choices that night,” just several days after he was released from prison: go to church with his wife or buy heroin and go out drinking. After hitting a strip club, Angel went to the Intown Bar, a local gay bar. There, he asked Alfred for a ride home.</p><p>Instead of taking him home, Angel claimed Alfred took him to Alfred’s home in Lincoln, just north of Providence, where Angel passed out. He claimed he woke up to find Moon fondling him. According to Angel, the two argued, and Angel struck Moon three times with a brass lamp. Angel was found guilty of second-degree murder as part of a plea agreement and was sentenced to 60 years in prison.</p><p>I made this photograph where the Intown Bar had been located. </p>", "lon": -71.412095, "lat": 41.8227, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -71.412095, "lat": 41.82269999999999, "range": 763.2984650151767, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/alfred_moon_mavica046.jpg", "still": "35_alfred_moon_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 50, "id": "0E0D7E1B8913D50AA360", "name": "Alfred Moon - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "q0UVp9tvzT4gmLlNBNtwxQ", "camera": {"lon": -71.41216246064918, "lat": 41.82279006564835, "alt": 6.468613179937168, "heading": 222.828608948647, "tilt": 80.95444703043124, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -71.41216246064918, "lat": 41.82279006564835, "for": "Alfred Moon", "label": "Alfred Moon - Street View", "still": "36_alfred_moon_street.jpg"}, {"index": 51, "id": "0719995A0E1176DC218A", "name": "Kareem Washington", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Passaic, New Jersey, Murdered, August 29, 1999", "html": "<p>Passaic, New Jersey, Murdered, August 29, 1999 </p><p>Kareem Washington’s mother said he “loved people and was very happy-go-lucky.” Kareem was found dead on Hanover Street by two workers from a nearby trucking company. Kareem had been stabbed several times in the back and throat. The coroner also said he sustained several “defensive” knife wounds.</p><p>Police and news reports reported that Kareem “lived alternately in Newark and Passaic.” The reports focused on the fact that Kareem was wearing women’s clothes and that the industrial area was known to police as a hangout for prostitutes. The police told news outlets that they believed he may have been a prostitute who was killed by his pimp. No evidence was found to support this theory, but the news never deviated from this narrative. The murderer was never found.</p><p>I made this photograph looking down the dead-end street where Kareem’s body was found. I was raised 40 minutes from this spot.</p>", "lon": -74.1383056, "lat": 40.8563889, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -74.1383056, "lat": 40.85638890000001, "range": 775.7757710281688, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/kareem_washington_mavica842.jpg", "still": "37_kareem_washington_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 52, "id": "0453CBA9E513D50C294F", "name": "Kareem Washington - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "bdlCigrsz9m0zgIB4dlPRw", "camera": {"lon": -74.13841516357796, "lat": 40.85650162433365, "alt": 38.89730791713125, "heading": 171.0655740949211, "tilt": 77.0760245751478, "fov": 59.9999882785815}, "lon": -74.13841516357796, "lat": 40.85650162433365, "for": "Kareem Washington", "label": "Kareem Washington - Street View", "still": "38_kareem_washington_street.jpg"}, {"index": 53, "id": "02D0BA55AB1176DCCC3C", "name": "Steen Fenrich", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Bayside, Queens, New York, Murdered Body Discovered March 21, 2000", "html": "<p>Bayside, Queens, New York, Murdered Body Discovered March 21, 2000 </p><p>Steen Fenrich joined the army and served nine months before he was discharged. In September 1999 he left his parents’ home in Dix Hills and was never heard of again. His parents did not file a missing persons report.</p><p>Six months later, a man strolling through Alley Pond Park found two plastic food containers, one of which contained human bones, part of a foot, part of a pelvis, and part of a skull. They were able to identify it as Steen’s skull because it was found with the phrase “gay nigger number one” scrawled on it along with his social security number.</p><p>After John, Steen’s stepfather, was told his son’s body was found, he called News 12 Long Island and told them his son was killed “because he was gay.” He also mentioned that Steen had been dismembered. The police had not yet made the dismemberment of Steen’s body public. The police went to question John. During questioning he fled, climbed on his roof, fired guns, and begged police to shoot him after declaring “I’m a failure as a father.” John committed suicide after an eight-hour standoff.</p><p>It was a cold, wintery day when I arrived at the lake. I felt my stomach turn. Every plastic piece of trash looked like a to-go container. I made this photograph beside the pond where parts of Steen’s body were found.</p><p><i>The New York Times</i> covered this fairly well.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2000/03/24/nyregion/signs-in-grisly-killing-point-to-bias-and-stepfather-who-killed-himself.html\">https://www.nytimes.com/2000/03/24/nyregion/signs-in-grisly-killing-point-to-bias-and-stepfather-who-killed-himself.html</a></p>", "lon": -73.7597778, "lat": 40.7590556, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -73.7597778, "lat": 40.7590556, "range": 777.02099147103, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/steen_fenrich_mavica109.jpg", "still": "39_steen_fenrich_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 54, "id": "070D6B36BC13D50EBB2E", "name": "Steen Fenrich - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "-gE9G7-JLyrbsqgDTp36Eg", "camera": {"lon": -73.75763952234706, "lat": 40.7601831226813, "alt": 5.800649501873116, "heading": 230.7055408678507, "tilt": 94.63547926732849, "fov": 59.99999452778832}, "lon": -73.75763952234706, "lat": 40.7601831226813, "for": "Steen Fenrich", "label": "Steen Fenrich - Street View", "still": "40_steen_fenrich_street.jpg"}, {"index": 55, "id": "0B78F075AA1176D2BB05", "name": "Chareka Keys", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Cleveland, Ohio, Murdered, September 27, 1999", "html": "<p>Cleveland, Ohio, Murdered, September 27, 1999 </p><p>Chareka Keys, a trans woman, was found on the loading dock of an abandoned factory in Cleveland. Police ruled her death a homicide but never arrested anyone for the crime. An African American queer youth group held a memorial service for her at Archwood United Church of Christ. I wasn’t able to find much about her life or the events that lead up to her murder.</p><p>I made this photograph from an empty lot across the street from the loading dock where her body was found. It was a quiet summer day. The only sounds I could hear were birds chirping.</p>", "lon": -81.64036953801387, "lat": 41.4959574700345, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -81.64037881776912, "lat": 41.49601654738616, "range": 42.22274864168867, "heading": 84.23760269594544, "tilt": 44.99538815762152}, "photo": "photos/chareka_keys.jpg", "still": "41_chareka_keys_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 56, "id": "05642362CE13D50F8E91", "name": "Chareka Keys - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "Wvm05RLjlND7Do-Yl5gpVg", "camera": {"lon": -81.64031515078898, "lat": 41.49614184864614, "alt": 205.8043353636481, "heading": 184.3512789229054, "tilt": 100.9328116140065, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -81.64031515078898, "lat": 41.49614184864614, "for": "Chareka Keys", "label": "Chareka Keys - Street View", "still": "42_chareka_keys_street.jpg"}, {"index": 57, "id": "009ACBDB5811FBC63358", "name": "Daniel O'Brien", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Denver, Colorado, Murdered, September 13, 1999", "html": "<p>Denver, Colorado, Murdered, September 13, 1999 </p><p>Samuel Grauman and Jason Arnold escaped from the Natrona County Jail in Casper, Wyoming. The two escapees stole a truck, drove to Denver, and went to a popular gay bar. They thought that gay men would be easy to rob. In the bar they picked up Daniel O’Brien and convinced him to take them back to his place. When they arrived at Daniel’s home they robbed him. During the robbery Samuel struck Daniel over the head with a flowerpot and began to choke him. Daniel died of heart problems precipitated by physical assault. Samuel then stole his car and credit cards.</p><p>Samuel was found in Nacogdoches, Texas, driving Daniel’s car. He also had Daniel’s wallet on him. He was sentenced to life in jail without parole.</p><p>Daniel’s family sued the jail for gross negligence, however, the court found that his death occurred too far away from the jail to be the fault of the jail.</p><p>“The escapees’ behavior in the cases which find that the negligence of the jail may be a proximate cause of harm occurred within the immediate vicinity of the jail. In this case, the murder of O’Brien occurred more than two days following Grauman's escape at a location approximately 280 miles from NCDC. There is simply no causal connection between the alleged negligence on the part of the county defendants and the murder of O’Brien. Reasonable minds could not disagree on this point.”</p><p>I made this photograph of Daniel’s apartment where he was murdered.</p>", "lon": -104.8921667, "lat": 39.6265833, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -104.8921667, "lat": 39.6265833, "range": 791.3503780410359, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/daniel_obrien_mavica498.jpg", "still": "43_daniel_o_brien_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 58, "id": "0A3E9AE49B13D511694C", "name": "Daniel O'Brien - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "rkasM6K_26nBDImM_V3lGg", "camera": {"lon": -104.8925644615276, "lat": 39.62618785246347, "alt": 1714.673706055388, "heading": 344.1012890045826, "tilt": 94.67811097947886, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -104.8925644615276, "lat": 39.62618785246347, "for": "Daniel O'Brien", "label": "Daniel O'Brien - Street View", "still": "44_daniel_o_brien_street.jpg"}, {"index": 59, "id": "0A9280E0A2117572350F", "name": "Sissy Bolden", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Savannah, Georgia, Murdered, November 20, 2000", "html": "<p>Savannah, Georgia, Murdered, November 20, 2000 </p><p>Sissy Bolden’s body was found dumped in the woods at the end of Pate Street in west Savannah in the Ogeecheeton neighborhood. Sissy was identified by police as a “man wearing women’s clothing who had a past criminal history, which included charges of solicitation of sodomy.” I wasn’t able to find much about her life and the events that lead up to her murder.</p><p>Edward Charles Wilkins Jr. was found guilty in 2007 for the murder of three individuals, including Sissy. The newspaper identified the victims as two women and a man dressed as a woman.</p><p>I made this image in the woods at the end of Pate Street where Sissy’s body was found. It was a rainy day and the street was quiet. I remember waving to a woman sitting on her porch nearby.</p>", "lon": -81.13130559999999, "lat": 32.0611389, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -81.13130559999999, "lat": 32.0611389, "range": 879.0289494240608, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/sissy_bolden_mavica107.jpg", "still": "45_sissy_bolden_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 60, "id": "077B87C52D13D512201B", "name": "Sissy Bolden - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "m96P9eiH7UXUQtMVgu3GnA", "camera": {"lon": -81.13115757017364, "lat": 32.0607147063172, "alt": 5.704703466917276, "heading": 325.8262997714714, "tilt": 92.55480417065341, "fov": 59.99946916892934}, "lon": -81.13115757017364, "lat": 32.0607147063172, "for": "Sissy Bolden", "label": "Sissy Bolden - Street View", "still": "46_sissy_bolden_street.jpg"}, {"index": 61, "id": "032B6DA54413CAFA649D", "name": "Allison Decatrel", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Inverness, Florida, Murdered, October 31, 1999", "html": "<p>Inverness, Florida, Murdered, October 31, 1999 </p><p>Allison and her high school friends, including Chris Elmore and Mark Maillet, went out on Halloween dressed in drag. The two boys dressed as girls, and the girls dressed as boys. When Richard Burzynski, a classmate, and some of his friends came across Allison and her group, they screamed homophobic slurs at them. The two groups had an argument and Richard left. He returned home to get a 1984 Buick sedan and a gun, with the intention of finding Allison’s group. Upon returning, he drove his car into the crowd, hitting Allison and Chris. He then backed up to run over Allison, who lay in the road. He shot at Mark. All the teenagers survived except for Allison. Richard was tried as an adult and eventually took a plea deal that reduced his sentence from life to 45 years.</p><p>I could not find any information about how Allison thought about herself. I don’t know if she would have identified as LGBTQIA, but she was certainly targeted because she was presenting in masculine clothing.</p><p>A year later many of Allison’s friends met on the same corner to erect a memorial.</p><p>I arrived in Inverness on a sunny day in June. I looked everywhere for the memorial. I thought I spotted it and tried to clear the dirt off the top of it, but it turned out to be a small rock. I never found the memorial.</p>", "lon": -82.32518, "lat": 28.80181169999999, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -82.32518, "lat": 28.8018117, "range": 912.1082675876105, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/allison_decatrel_mavica012.jpg", "still": "47_allison_decatrel_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 62, "id": "0DBF31629C13D514B139", "name": "Allison Decatrel - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "Q9y8NxzD8L2HDbYzCwiIOQ", "camera": {"lon": -82.32515094581314, "lat": 28.80185183157176, "alt": 29.84414029660021, "heading": 327.9719089434188, "tilt": 74.81989617029717, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -82.32515094581314, "lat": 28.80185183157176, "for": "Allison Decatrel", "label": "Allison Decatrel - Street View", "still": "48_allison_decatrel_street.jpg"}, {"index": 63, "id": "025BCC9B341176CD29CC", "name": "Tacy Raino Ranta", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Baltimore, Maryland, Murdered, November 22, 1999", "html": "<p>Baltimore, Maryland, Murdered, November 22, 1999 </p><p>Tacy Raino Ranta was shot in the chest just one block from her home outside Baltimore. She was walking to the European Union, her favorite neighborhood bar. She was an activist and the leader of Tran*quility, a Baltimore transgender support group active in the Metropolitan Community Church of Baltimore. Tacy lobbied in Annapolis on hate crimes bills as a spokesperson for It’s Time, Maryland!, a transgender rights organization.</p><p>Jessica Xavier of It’s Time, America! said “All of us in ITA understand the risks we take by just living our lives, let alone doing the political grassroots work that simply must be done. Tacy gladly accepted those risks and courageously stepped forward to right the wrongs so many of us encounter on a daily basis.”</p><p>Police claimed there was no evidence the shooting was a hate crime and instead linked it to a local robbery spree. The police determined nothing was taken, and none of the other 12 victims in the supposedly related robberies were shot. Both police and news reports used male pronouns and described her as a “man in a dress.”</p><p>I made this photograph on the block where she was murdered.</p>", "lon": -76.56553020616553, "lat": 39.32749013137281, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -76.56575406654291, "lat": 39.32742861206926, "range": 98.97737694116586, "heading": 146.8316103622938, "tilt": 44.99557375366407}, "photo": "photos/tacy_raino_ranta_mavica334.jpg", "still": "49_tacy_raino_ranta_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 64, "id": "0B341B171613D515A113", "name": "Tacy Raino Ranta - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "B1FmJK9W4lzGJ1truR6_Sw", "camera": {"lon": -76.56572360359121, "lat": 39.32756302229437, "alt": 38.14306259155273, "heading": 108.4665534423319, "tilt": 88.1946023925929, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -76.56572360359121, "lat": 39.32756302229437, "for": "Tacy Raino Ranta", "label": "Tacy Raino Ranta - Street View", "still": "50_tacy_raino_ranta_street.jpg"}, {"index": 65, "id": "0FFF82EAD31176D3BC8F", "name": "Unnamed Intersex Infant", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Dallas, Texas, Murdered, December 8, 1999", "html": "<p>Dallas, Texas, Murdered, December 8, 1999 </p><p>An unnamed intersex three-day-old infant was murdered. The body showed signs of blunt-force trauma to the head, as well as strangulation. The infant’s parents reported the murder. Gangaudaya Kavali, the infant’s father, claimed that when he was at the store his wife, Aruna, left their infant on the couch while she stepped into the bathroom. She claimed that while she was in the bathroom a stranger entered their apartment, stole the infant, and threw the infant outside.</p><p>A Collin County Medical Examiner found shards of glass in the baby’s esophagus and small intestine, indicating that someone initially tried to kill the child by forcing them to eat glass, in an effort to cause internal bleeding. Aruna was eventually found guilty of murdering her three-day-old child.</p><p>The murder was not tried as a hate crime. The media repeatedly identified the child as male and referred to the child’s genitalia as being deformed.</p><p>When I arrived at the apartment complex that the Kavali family lived in when the murder took place, it was raining. Suddenly, the rain broke and a rainbow appeared in the sky over their building. I made this picture in front of the home where the infant was murdered.</p>", "lon": -96.7717778, "lat": 32.9898333, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -96.7717778, "lat": 32.9898333, "range": 869.0637152696535, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/unnamed_infant_mavica435.jpg", "still": "51_unnamed_intersex_infant_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 66, "id": "0B72ED00881BF56C8014", "name": "Unnamed Intersex Infant - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "iyjJ3oCQ7lhHEEaG7R798w", "camera": {"lon": -96.77014949115389, "lat": 32.98797975646043, "alt": 210.3253944379621, "heading": 354.7221228030244, "tilt": 79.12505075878937, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -96.77014949115389, "lat": 32.98797975646043, "for": "Unnamed Intersex Infant", "label": "Unnamed Intersex Infant - Street View", "still": "52_unnamed_intersex_infant_street.jpg"}, {"index": 67, "id": "0F6400CEBA13D5B01EA3", "name": "Jamie Ray Tolbert", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Mobile County, Alabama, Tortured and Murdered, Date Unknown", "html": "<p>Mobile County, Alabama, Tortured and Murdered, Date Unknown </p><p>Jamie Ray Tolbert was a certified emergency medical technician and worked as a safety supervisor for an offshore drilling contractor. On New Year’s Eve 1999, Jamie’s younger brother tied Jamie’s bowtie, then Jamie headed to Joey’s on the Beach, a gay club in Biloxi, in a tuxedo. At 24, this was the first time he'd worn a tux. He hadn't gone to his high school prom.</p><p>Jamie was abducted by Brent David Kabat and Jeremy Shawn Bentley in the parking lot of Joey’s and left in Jamie’s new Nissan Xterra. Jeremy choked Jamie with Rollerblade laces and slammed him into the ground, then killed him by strangling him with a belt. Jamie’s body was eventually found in the woods in Alabama.</p><p>Bentley and another man, David Kabat, went on a cross-country spending spree using Jamie’s credit card and were arrested in California two weeks later, still driving Jamie’s Nissan Xterra.</p><p>Equality Mississippi was founded in response to Jamie’s murder.</p><p>I made this photograph on the spot where Joey's on the Beach had been. Now it is a field. </p>", "lon": -88.9412217, "lat": 30.3947217, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -88.9412217, "lat": 30.3947217, "range": 896.3156913246714, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/jamie_ray_tolbert_mvc-022s.jpg", "still": "53_jamie_ray_tolbert_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 68, "id": "0CF702446A13D5B30CB8", "name": "Jamie Ray Tolbert - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "x3dyIUph1C8nh9Z5QRtuFA", "camera": {"lon": -88.94122987773406, "lat": 30.39472820065507, "alt": 8.253897965442354, "heading": 154.7673703575644, "tilt": 88.73419521980354, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -88.94122987773406, "lat": 30.39472820065507, "for": "Jamie Ray Tolbert", "label": "Jamie Ray Tolbert - Street View", "still": "54_jamie_ray_tolbert_street.jpg"}, {"index": 69, "id": "060616D36E13CAF4FCFE", "name": "Alina Marie Barragan", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "San Jose, California, Murdered, January 18, 2000", "html": "<p>San José, California, Murdered, January 18, 2000 </p><p>Alina Marie Barragan, a 19-year-old trans woman living in San José, was loved and accepted by her family and the other families that lived in her neighborhood. She volunteered at the Billy DeFrank LGBTQ+ Community Center. On January 18, she met Kozi Santino Scott after work at a bus stop or bar (I have heard both). Kozi invited Alina back to his place for a drink. They drank and talked and screwed around a bit. Kozi wanted to have sex, and Alina did not. They got into an argument about Alina’s gender. Kozi refused to acknowledge Alina as a woman and attacked her, eventually strangling her and killing her.</p><p>Kozi called a friend, saying, “I got a dead body in the living room. This shit got me mad, man, and I taught him a lesson.” Kozi asked a friend for help disposing of the body but was refused. Kozi wrapped Alina’s body in some sheets and a blanket and hid her in the trunk of his 1981 Cadillac. Kozi’s friend called the police, who used a crowbar to open the trunk of Kozi’s car. There, they found Alina’s body, and Kozi confessed to the murder. During his confession, he referred to Alina neither as “him” nor “her,” but “it.”</p><p>The prosecution attempted to try the case as a first-degree murder and hate crime. Kozi was convicted of second-degree murder and sentenced to 15 years in prison. The jury felt that they could not prove Kozi murdered Alina because of her gender. Even though Alina had legally changed her name, almost all the press at the time referred to her by her birth name and repeatedly misgendered her.</p><p>I made this photograph from the street across from Kozi's house where police found Alina's body in his trunk. </p>", "lon": -121.8409283, "lat": 37.345355, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -121.8409283, "lat": 37.345355, "range": 819.3011335397553, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/alina_marie_marragan_mavica121.jpg", "still": "55_alina_marie_barragan_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 70, "id": "0CE14BF7FE13D5B47B37", "name": "Alina Marie Barragan - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "mCFwcx7Am1BFjDCV8vl4qg", "camera": {"lon": -121.8409705001883, "lat": 37.34547942511702, "alt": 31.50414466837629, "heading": 30.19800570246445, "tilt": 93.30893491118142, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -121.8409705001883, "lat": 37.34547942511702, "for": "Alina Marie Barragan", "label": "Alina Marie Barragan - Street View", "still": "56_alina_marie_barragan_street.jpg"}, {"index": 71, "id": "0DA94473051176DAD7AB", "name": "Henry Stuart Matis", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Los Altos, California, Suicide, February 25, 2000", "html": "<p>Los Altos, California, Suicide, February 25, 2000 </p><p>Henry Stuart Matis, a 32-year-old gay man and devout member of the Church of Latter-Day Saints, pinned a note to his chest that said “Do not resuscitate” and shot himself on the steps of his church. Having realized he was gay by age 7, Stuart had long struggled with the church’s position on homosexuality as he tried to reconcile his being gay with his faith. The church regards homosexuality as a sin, advocating that gays and lesbians be celibate for life, and its involvement with California’s Proposition 22 (which recognized marriage only between a man and a woman) particularly disturbed Stuart. He came out to his parents the year before he killed himself, but despite their support, he felt he had to choose between “life” or being Christian.</p><p>In his suicide letter, he wrote, “Mother, Dad, and family. I have committed suicide…. I engaged my mind in a false dilemma: either one was gay or one was Christian. As I believed I was Christian, I believed I could never be gay.”</p><p>I took this image in front of the church where Stuart killed himself.</p><p>Stuart wrote a very extensive letter to his cousin Clay before he committed suicide.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://hfarg.wordpress.com/index-2/chapter-3-doctrines-and-policies/stuart-matis-letter-to-his-cousin/\">https://hfarg.wordpress.com/index-2/chapter-3-doctrines-and-policies/stuart-matis-letter-to-his-cousin/</a></p>", "lon": -122.0780278, "lat": 37.3593889, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -122.0780278, "lat": 37.35938890000001, "range": 819.1330209852289, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/henry_stuart_matis_mavica773.jpg", "still": "57_henry_stuart_matis_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 72, "id": "023D7F3BB613D5B5C8C6", "name": "Henry Stuart Matis - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "DegW9FQvSMIV1zQ54RbagA", "camera": {"lon": -122.0777738508407, "lat": 37.35948839988659, "alt": 58.37669804667046, "heading": 234.8681590361655, "tilt": 101.7425332122037, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -122.0777738508407, "lat": 37.35948839988659, "for": "Henry Stuart Matis", "label": "Henry Stuart Matis - Street View", "still": "58_henry_stuart_matis_street.jpg"}, {"index": 73, "id": "0115968C591176C93504", "name": "Brian David James Hyer", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Kingman, Arizona, Suicide, March 3, 2000", "html": "<p>Kingman, Arizona, Suicide, March 3, 2000 </p><p>Known as DJ, Hyer was a strong advocate for gay rights. He was proud to be gay and proud to be Mormon, connecting with his religion through Affirmation and Reconciliation, both groups for gay and lesbian Mormons. Years after coming out, he was still attending that ward and discussing gay issues with his bishop.</p><p>DJ lived in many places and tried very hard to find happiness. He loved French culture and once traveled to Paris as an artist’s assistant. He loved movies, paintings, and art. He loved hiking, nature, and animals, especially cats. He loved to grow flowers and was a talented cook.</p><p>At 32 DJ committed suicide, upset with the Mormon church’s involvement with California’s Proposition 22, which recognized marriage only between a man and a woman.  DJ was also distraught by the suicide of Stuart Matis, another 32-year-old gay Mormon, who shot himself outside a Mormon temple and was conflicted about the church’s official stand on homosexuality.</p><p>Affirmation held a memorial service for DJ and Stuart at St. Mark’s Episcopal Cathedral in Salt Lake City. DJ had sent a suicide letter to his close friends and parts of it were read: “It is unfortunate that the lives of good people such as Stuart Matis, Matthew Shepard, and many others go unnoticed. I see Proposition 22 as a last straw in my life-long battle to see peace in the world I live in.”</p><p>I made this photograph in Salt Lake City. This is the mountain view across the street from his gravesite.</p>", "lon": -111.9158056, "lat": 40.5592778, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -111.9158056, "lat": 40.5592778, "range": 779.5701104039659, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/brian_david_james_hyer_mavica294.jpg", "still": "59_brian_david_james_hyer_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 74, "id": "0BB027F8D313D5B73320", "name": "Brian David James Hyer - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "JDAkKYGxQx3f_Q3Zj-uvzA", "camera": {"lon": -111.9158095542169, "lat": 40.55922116801597, "alt": 1336.403688783812, "heading": 228.6143556083802, "tilt": 89.83598204162573, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -111.9158095542169, "lat": 40.55922116801597, "for": "Brian David James Hyer", "label": "Brian David James Hyer - Street View", "still": "60_brian_david_james_hyer_street.jpg"}, {"index": 75, "id": "010CDB89DC13CB33C472", "name": "Clay Whitmer", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "San Francisco, Suicide, March 20, 2000", "html": "<p>San Francisco, Suicide, March 20, 2000 </p><p>Clay Douglass Whitmer was raised Mormon. He was intelligent and had very high aspirations. Clay committed suicide in the San Francisco Bay Area on March 20, 2000, three weeks after his friend Stuart Matis took his own life. Stuart was Clay’s very dear friend. They went to Brigham Young University together and served on Mormon missions together in Italy.</p><p>The death of Stuart may have been the last straw in Clay’s life. He wrote in his suicide note that he wanted to say goodbye to all of his friends and apologize for the pain he knew he would cause them. For his final place, he chose a location that his friends had no particular connection to. Peculiar as it may sound, even while contemplating his own death he thought of others. This was the type of person he was.</p>", "lon": -122.4415705, "lat": 37.786408, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -122.4415705, "lat": 37.786408, "range": 813.9947007194072, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/clay_whitmer_mvc-001s.jpg", "still": "61_clay_whitmer_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 76, "id": "062BD7B26E13D5B859E5", "name": "Clay Whitmer - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "fife-media:AF1QipMG1RoX-hDu7AOeuvKh6yhr78bJzFvgr2lGxxg1", "camera": {"lon": -122.441884484256, "lat": 37.7863616729507, "alt": 52.62980705263122, "heading": 192.2011037199442, "tilt": 103.4140327012109, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -122.441884484256, "lat": 37.7863616729507, "for": "Clay Whitmer", "label": "Clay Whitmer - Street View", "still": "62_clay_whitmer_street.jpg"}, {"index": 77, "id": "0F4E8F518F1DDB1641B4", "name": "Stephanie Yazum", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Schenectady, New York, Murdered, March 23, 2000", "html": "<p>Schenectady, New York, Murdered, March 23, 2000</p><p>Stephanie Yazum was a trans woman living in Schenectady, New York. Stephanie was close to her family. Her parents picked her up for church every Sunday. Just after 4 a.m. on March 23rd, neighbors reported screaming from Stephanie’s apartment. When the police arrived, they found her on her floor bleeding from a deep slash in her throat. She was pronounced dead later that evening. The police labeled this a crime based on her “lifestyle.” Friends believed that she may have picked up her murderer at her favorite local bar, which closed at 4 a.m., and brought him home only to be murdered. Her murderer was caught and found guilty, but in court papers and local news coverage she was deadnamed and it was never acknowledged that she lived her personal life as Stephanie.</p>", "lon": -73.94888329999999, "lat": 42.81762, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -73.94888329999999, "lat": 42.81762000000001, "range": 750.2375369471644, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/stephanie_yazum_mavica_005.jpg", "still": "63_stephanie_yazum_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 78, "id": "0177DA15741DDB17DA5F", "name": "Stephanie Yazum - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "c8ltBxFDEO1bKV5h0VdENw", "camera": {"lon": -73.94905042667811, "lat": 42.81780728172987, "alt": 73.58582234653224, "heading": 283.7463140941133, "tilt": 105.5663653863965, "fov": 59.99998153492268}, "lon": -73.94905042667811, "lat": 42.81780728172987, "for": "Stephanie Yazum", "label": "Stephanie Yazum - Street View", "still": "64_stephanie_yazum_street.jpg"}, {"index": 79, "id": "0E91A973BC13CB3C1454", "name": "Déjà", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Miami, Florida, Murdered, April 20, 2000", "html": "<p>Miami, Florida, Murdered, April 20, 2000 </p><p>Déjà was a beautiful trans woman who performed in Miami clubs and was a sex worker. She often hung out with the other girls at 79th Street and Biscayne. Jeffrey Trivanty Flanders was barely four feet tall and bowlegged. He was nicknamed Bowlegs and carried a large gun that he liked to flaunt.</p><p>On April 20, Bowlegs offered to pay Déjà $40 for oral sex. They went behind a nearby building, and when Déjà got on her knees Bowlegs pulled out a gun and said, “Give me your purse.” She responded with “Hell, no,” and turned to run away. He shot her in the back and stole her purse. Déjà’s friend, Precious, and many of the girls heard the gunshot and saw Bowlegs fleeing on a bike with the purse.</p><p>The accounts I found mentioned that another worker named Pilar had been killed four months earlier, but I was unable to find any information about her. Precious was against Déjà’s colleagues going to the police with their information. “We said, ‘It’s time for us to start sticking together because the police do not care. Look how they treated Pilar. What makes you think they’ll do anything about Déjà?’”</p><p>Precious and the girls began systematically visiting all the known locations where Bowlegs lived or hung out, and eventually they tracked him down. They sped to a police station and reported everything to the officer who met them. The police immediately sent a squad, surrounded Bowlegs, and arrested him.</p><p>He eventually confessed to the murder but claimed his gun went off accidentally.</p><p>I made this photograph on the corner of 79th Street and Biscayne where she and the girls like to hang out.</p><p><i>Miami New Times</i> really does this story justice:</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.miaminewtimes.com/news/death-of-a-maiden-6355507\">https://www.miaminewtimes.com/news/death-of-a-maiden-6355507</a></p>", "lon": -80.18446329999999, "lat": 25.8471533, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -80.18446329999999, "lat": 25.8471533, "range": 939.442545629445, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "still": "65_de_ja_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 80, "id": "0F2DC671BD13D5B926E6", "name": "Déjà - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "-YJLGqbUuGkESGMwZiN3Aw", "camera": {"lon": -80.1844780147917, "lat": 25.84711624551046, "alt": 3.824900150299072, "heading": 318.4478031572403, "tilt": 109.645666959621, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -80.1844780147917, "lat": 25.84711624551046, "for": "Déjà", "label": "Déjà - Street View", "still": "66_de_ja_street.jpg"}, {"index": 81, "id": "0B69CEBEF61176CEBAC1", "name": "Tyra Henderson", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Washington, DC, Murdered, April 23, 2000", "html": "<p>Washington, DC, Murdered, April 23, 2000 </p><p>Tyra Henderson, a 22-year-old trans woman, was found beaten to death in an alleyway in Washington, DC. I wasn’t able to find any information about Tyra’s homicide case or her life.</p><p>A complaint was filed against the police because it took authorities five hours to respond to a 911 call reporting sounds of a person moaning in the alleyway where Tyra’s body was found. The complainant sought help from the ACLU. As of today, the complaint is still listed as being “under review” by police. Tyra's murderer was never found.</p><p>I made this photograph in the alleyway where Tyra’s body was found.</p>", "lon": -77.02905559999999, "lat": 38.9350278, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -77.02905559999999, "lat": 38.9350278, "range": 799.9546255670812, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/tyra_henderson_mavica366-edit.jpg", "still": "67_tyra_henderson_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 82, "id": "02C6BA63F713D5BBF0EC", "name": "Tyra Henderson - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "esJxceSAK7iMGHUJLXxu_w", "camera": {"lon": -77.02913633247965, "lat": 38.93494767371872, "alt": 54.83225250244141, "heading": 32.1573061052479, "tilt": 88.10754289765075, "fov": 59.99999999041677}, "lon": -77.02913633247965, "lat": 38.93494767371872, "for": "Tyra Henderson", "label": "Tyra Henderson - Street View", "still": "68_tyra_henderson_street.jpg"}, {"index": 83, "id": "0DDACC3F321176CAE19B", "name": "Carla Natasha Hunt", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Suitland, Maryland, Murdered, May 15, 2000", "html": "<p>Suitland, Maryland, Murdered, May 15, 2000 </p><p>Carla Natasha Hunt, a 35-year-old trans woman, was found shot to death in the foyer of her apartment. She was the second trans woman to be found dead in the Washington, DC, area in 2000. The police did not pursue either death as a hate crime. The police repeatedly claimed to know that both women frequented a section of the city known as a gathering place for “transsexual persons who solicit sex for money.” There was no evidence that either one of them engaged in sex work.</p><p>I made this photograph of the bush directly in front of her apartment door.</p>", "lon": -76.9479722, "lat": 38.8607778, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -76.9479722, "lat": 38.86077779999999, "range": 800.8717432703864, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/carla_natasha_hunt_mavica334-edit.jpg", "still": "69_carla_natasha_hunt_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 84, "id": "02974A759713D5BF0B8C", "name": "Carla Natasha Hunt - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "7iQAJk5wEDe39lgObFXNXg", "camera": {"lon": -76.94837009313089, "lat": 38.86040780561633, "alt": 56.98904141978934, "heading": 38.90885072555814, "tilt": 91.14269147799664, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -76.94837009313089, "lat": 38.86040780561633, "for": "Carla Natasha Hunt", "label": "Carla Natasha Hunt - Street View", "still": "70_carla_natasha_hunt_street.jpg"}, {"index": 85, "id": "01F424E98E1176C62392", "name": "Steven Larry Wheeler", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Salt Lake City, Utah, Suicide, May 27, 2000", "html": "<p>Salt Lake City, Utah, Suicide, May 27, 2000 </p><p>Steven Larry Wheeler committed suicide after the Mormon Church supported California’s Proposition 22, the anti-gay-marriage constitutional amendment.</p><p>Steve’s note to family: “To Everyone: I cannot fight depression any longer. I have had to fight depression all my life, although for most of my life I had no idea what was wrong. Nothing works. Anti-depression drugs, counseling, mood enhancement drugs, supplements—nothing lifts the darkness. Churchill called it the “Black Dog.” It is more like the black death. Fifty years is long enough. The depression grows deeper the older I get. I can see no way out. I am going to a place where there is no darkness, no pain, and no sadness. I am truly sorry. Please forgive me. —Steven L. Wheeler”</p><p>I made this photograph in front of Steven's home. </p>", "lon": -111.8682335698484, "lat": 40.76967027327989, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -111.8681962064819, "lat": 40.76957319314995, "range": 130.3276643775826, "heading": 60.20065180625863, "tilt": 44.18232289056069}, "photo": "photos/steven_larry_wheeler_mavica263.jpg", "still": "71_steven_larry_wheeler_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 86, "id": "04B5186D6613D5C05470", "name": "Steven Larry Wheeler - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "80MWIRdr6pqY3tC5PthYSQ", "camera": {"lon": -111.8683579755279, "lat": 40.76927443714988, "alt": 1345.578210194508, "heading": 10.48303197861181, "tilt": 102.0734970589369, "fov": 59.99823537060608}, "lon": -111.8683579755279, "lat": 40.76927443714988, "for": "Steven Larry Wheeler", "label": "Steven Larry Wheeler - Street View", "still": "72_steven_larry_wheeler_street.jpg"}, {"index": 87, "id": "0F4D6FB8E613CB569C72", "name": "Jamie L. Brown", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Longboat Key, Florida, Murdered, June 8, 2000", "html": "<p>Longboat Key, Florida, Murdered, June 8, 2000 </p><p>Jamie Brown was a theater director with experience in both Broadway and regional companies.</p><p>On June 8, 2000, authorities discovered Jamie’s body at his home. They found him face down on his living room floor with more than a dozen stab wounds.</p><p>A bloody handprint on a lamp led police to Jameson Smith, a local handyman. Jameson later confessed to the murder. He claimed Jamie had asked him to come over to look at a drain. Jameson said Jamie made a pass at him and in self-defense he stabbed him.</p><p>I made this photograph looking across the street from his beautiful home on the water in Longboat Key. The day was warm, the sun was bright, and the wind felt amazing. </p>", "lon": -82.6379917, "lat": 27.3876583, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -82.6379917, "lat": 27.38765829999999, "range": 925.5135691682424, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/jamie_l_brownmvc-005s.jpg", "still": "73_jamie_l_brown_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 88, "id": "0DEBABDD0313D5C18D96", "name": "Jamie L. Brown - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "1ub1gmZt9XU95EXIGrC42w", "camera": {"lon": -82.6379467078779, "lat": 27.3878666472797, "alt": 3.003353694262147, "heading": 156.8721116863895, "tilt": 86.67317807873334, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -82.6379467078779, "lat": 27.3878666472797, "for": "Jamie L. Brown", "label": "Jamie L. Brown - Street View", "still": "74_jamie_l_brown_street.jpg"}, {"index": 89, "id": "0C23986AD013CB00E7A8", "name": "Amanda Milan", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "New York City, New York, Murdered, June 20, 2000", "html": "<p>New York City, New York, Murdered, June 20, 2000 </p><p>Amanda Milan was a trans woman who traveled a lot to Milan and Paris and London, where she worked as a high-class escort and hung with a fashionable crowd.</p><p>The night she was killed she had gone to meet friends in Times Square for a coffee at McDonald’s on Eighth and 43rd. She left around 4 a.m. and headed across the street to catch a cab. As she walked, Dwayne McCuller yelled at her, \"Get your fucking drag queen ass away from me. I know what you have between your legs.\"</p><p>Amanda was the kind of girl who stood up for herself. She and Dwayne exchanged words, and Amanda walked away. Eugene Celestine witnessed this exchange and yelled, \"Yo, I got a knife.\" Dwayne said, \"Give it to me.\"</p><p>He grabbed the knife and chased her across the street as her friends watched and screamed to warn her. He jumped on Amanda and shoved the knife into her throat. She collapsed and began to bleed out in front of Duane Reade. </p><p>I made this photograph on the corner of 43rd and Eighth. It was a cold day in December, and I struggled to battle tourists rushing by.</p><p>Gay Hate Crimes Wiki does a great job with this story:</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"http://gayhistory.wikidot.com/amanda-milan\">http://gayhistory.wikidot.com/amanda-milan</a></p>", "lon": -73.98953227268584, "lat": 40.7573781754238, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -73.98961673990175, "lat": 40.75720896603261, "range": 202.4070548313466, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/amanda_milan_mavica143.jpg", "still": "75_amanda_milan_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 90, "id": "0C11DAAC4D13D5C43FB0", "name": "Amanda Milan - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "bqZs9oNpdyW4WOHyuaX0vQ", "camera": {"lon": -73.98964480335283, "lat": 40.7574863162536, "alt": 13.17154598236084, "heading": 262.9560549247878, "tilt": 96.48352055913107, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -73.98964480335283, "lat": 40.7574863162536, "for": "Amanda Milan", "label": "Amanda Milan - Street View", "still": "76_amanda_milan_street.jpg"}, {"index": 91, "id": "05947782E711756F4FF7", "name": "JR Warren", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Grant Town, West Virginia, Murdered, July 4, 2000", "html": "<p>Grant Town, West Virginia, Murdered, July 4, 2000 </p><p>JR Warren grew up in Grant Town, West Virginia, a small rural town of just over 600 residents. He was described as a soft-spoken, mild-mannered, and generous young man by those who knew him. He had severe learning disabilities and was born with several fingers missing on one hand.</p><p>When JR was a teenager he came out to his mother, Brenda, and his reverend at Mount Beulah Baptist Church. With the help of Reverend Staples, his mother came to what she believed was a moral compromise with her son about his sexual orientation. She believed that “God never said we have the right to judge and punish one another about our personal lives. He said to leave the judging up to him. We are only required to love one another. If we don’t love one another like God loves us, we will never see heaven.”</p><p>JR left his home for a late-night walk on July 3, 2000. He was 26. He dropped by a house that was being painted by some acquaintances: David Allen Parker, Jared Wilson, and Jason Shoemaker, all teenagers. David claimed that JR had spread rumors that they were having a sexual relationship, and he and Jared attacked JR, pummeling and kicking him to the floor while wearing steel-toed boots. Shoemaker witnessed the beating but didn’t try to stop it. The three teenagers then dragged JR, who was still conscious, into a car and drove him to a deserted stretch of road at the edge of town. They dumped him in the middle of the road and ran over him four times in an attempt to disguise his massive injuries as the result of a hit-and-run.</p><p>The Marion County Sheriff claimed he had no evidence that JR’s killing was a hate crime, primarily because West Virginia’s law didn’t cover it as such. A few weeks before JR was killed, West Virginia’s legislature failed to act on a bill that would have included sexual orientation in the state’s hate crimes statutes. If the bill had passed, it would have taken effect three days before JR’s murder.</p><p>It was a beautiful, sunny summer day when I arrived in Grant Town. I made this photograph looking down the hill in front of the house JR was nearly beaten to death in. I watched two young boys, one on a bike and the other on foot, engaged in what appeared to be friendly banter as they went down the hill.</p><p>Wikipedia does a good job of the details of the case. </p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Arthur_Warren\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_Arthur_Warren</a></p>", "lon": -80.18011109999999, "lat": 39.55930559999999, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -80.18011109999999, "lat": 39.55930559999999, "range": 792.1923532885208, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/jr_warren_mavica036-edit.jpg", "still": "77_jr_warren_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 92, "id": "070F8476D81BF5722A5B", "name": "JR Warren - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "oMCKD18ivEARw3uT0Y1Gsg", "camera": {"lon": -80.17746451332768, "lat": 39.555779224594, "alt": 296.711681356596, "heading": 283.9886661752575, "tilt": 86.54743475218375, "fov": 59.99999999311301}, "lon": -80.17746451332768, "lat": 39.555779224594, "for": "JR Warren", "label": "JR Warren - Street View", "still": "78_jr_warren_street.jpg"}, {"index": 93, "id": "0AEFD3617613D5F72C5A", "name": "Julia Carrizales", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Webster, Texas, Murdered, July 21, 2000", "html": "<p>Webster, Texas, Murdered, July 21, 2000 </p><p>Julia Carrizales was a 25-year-old from South Houston. She was choked to death and then stabbed by Kelvin Antonio Navarro. Kelvin was 18 at the time and he enlisted a 15-year-old friend to help him dump Julia’s body outside of Nasa in Webster, Texas. By the time her body was discovered, it was so badly decomposed that it took several days to identify Julia.</p><p>The media identified Julia by her birth name and referred to her as a “man in drag.” There is very little information on her available.</p><p>I made this photograph approximately where her body was found. </p>", "lon": -95.11449912381954, "lat": 29.53406604592912, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -95.11428091591246, "lat": 29.53387959268665, "range": 432.2506392600191, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/julia_carrizales_mavica797.jpg", "still": "79_julia_carrizales_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 94, "id": "070E2943E613D5F9A69C", "name": "Julia Carrizales - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "Hep_rjXWmJWqNg5e93TFtg", "camera": {"lon": -95.11464745321125, "lat": 29.53392152102021, "alt": 4.719808700548627, "heading": 335.9871467488558, "tilt": 82.21059907504444, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -95.11464745321125, "lat": 29.53392152102021, "for": "Julia Carrizales", "label": "Julia Carrizales - Street View", "still": "80_julia_carrizales_street.jpg"}, {"index": 95, "id": "0D87FC578613CB3929BA", "name": "Danny Overstreet", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Roanoke, Virginia, Murdered, September 22, 2000", "html": "<p>Roanoke, Virginia, Murdered, September 22, 2000 </p><p>Danny Lee Overstreet worked for Verizon in customer service and had a beautician's license. He enjoyed perming his mother’s hair. He brought his poodle, Friday, with him everywhere he went. One Friday night, Danny was at the Backstreet Cafe, a local gay bar, when former marine Ronald Gay walked into the bar and opened fire. He shot Danny in the chest, killing him and wounding six other people. Before the shooting, Ronald drank a bottle of whisky at a local bar, asked where the local gay bar was, and said he wanted to go “waste some faggots.” After leaving the bar, Ronald tossed aside his gun because he didn't want to hurt any policeman. He claimed that he had been taunted his entire life for his last name and hated it.</p><p>I made this photograph on the site where the Backstreet Cafe used to be. It's now a bar called the Front Row.</p>", "lon": -79.94821999999999, "lat": 37.2723617, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -79.94821999999999, "lat": 37.2723617, "range": 820.1747424014717, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/danny_overstreet_mavica066.jpg", "still": "81_danny_overstreet_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 96, "id": "0B196FAED313D5C8A07F", "name": "Danny Overstreet - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "BFidvFH7UntMkNF8To0xrg", "camera": {"lon": -79.94816302825281, "lat": 37.2722967583918, "alt": 287.4065856939284, "heading": 161.9753703245755, "tilt": 86.77794732305466, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -79.94816302825281, "lat": 37.2722967583918, "for": "Danny Overstreet", "label": "Danny Overstreet - Street View", "still": "82_danny_overstreet_street.jpg"}, {"index": 97, "id": "09237949E9117577B571", "name": "Steve Ruck", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Cedaredge, Colorado, Murdered, October 6, 2000", "html": "<p>Cedaredge, Colorado, Murdered, October 6, 2000 </p><p>John Moore and Steve Ruck lived in a small trailer park in Cedaredge, Colorado. Ruck befriended their new neighbor, Bob Wells, and on October 6 Moore, Steve, and Bob went fishing and played golf. Later that evening Steve went over to Bob’s trailer to hang out.</p><p>When John, Steve’s partner, arrived home he walked outside Bob’s trailer and could hear the two men’s voices growing loud inside. He looked in Bob’s kitchen and saw the two men peeling apples. Bob asked Steve if he wanted to see his guns, and the two men walked back to a bedroom.</p><p>John followed them along the outside of the trailer and heard Steve say to Bob three times, “Don’t point that gun at me.” John ran to get help, but by the time he returned to the trailer, Steve was dead.</p><p>After the shooting, Bob walked to another trailer and told another neighbor, Ernie Johnson, “Call 911. I just shot a guy.” Ernie told the authorities this information on the night of the shooting, but the police never contacted him again.</p><p>Bob changed his story several times over the following week or two. A few weeks after the shooting, Bob moved to Ohio. In February 2001 Steve’s death was ruled a suicide.</p><p>Steve’s obituary said, “Steve’s friend and roommate, John Moore, of Cedaredge, survives and was very grateful to share Steve’s life.”</p><p>I made this photograph of the side of Bob’s house where John would have listened to the last moments of his partner’s life.</p>", "lon": -107.9272222, "lat": 38.8939167, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -107.9272222, "lat": 38.8939167, "range": 800.4625810288737, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/steve_ruck_mavica187.jpg", "still": "83_steve_ruck_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 98, "id": "0D6829C78513D5CB37EB", "name": "Steve Ruck - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "WOju28nhXz7VG8AbvqAwqg", "camera": {"lon": -107.9274374520677, "lat": 38.89396464851322, "alt": 1876.9081534443, "heading": 107.9981150626186, "tilt": 87.35835591239962, "fov": 59.99924300909237}, "lon": -107.9274374520677, "lat": 38.89396464851322, "for": "Steve Ruck", "label": "Steve Ruck - Street View", "still": "84_steve_ruck_street.jpg"}, {"index": 99, "id": "0E6B63958113CB04F761", "name": "Ana Melisa Cortez", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Nashville, Tennessee, Murdered, October 15, 2000", "html": "<p>Nashville, Tennessee, Murdered, October 15, 2000 </p><p>Ana was found dead in her home at age 20. She had been stabbed multiple times. The only information about her that I was able to find was an article that misgendered her, identifying her by her birth name and saying she had “carried out his day-to-day activities dressed as a man.”</p><p>I made this photograph outside her home where she was found dead.</p>", "lon": -86.72386999999999, "lat": 36.0751278, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -86.72386999999999, "lat": 36.0751278, "range": 834.3150326181214, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/ana_melissa_cortz_mvc-014s.jpg", "still": "85_ana_melisa_cortez_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 100, "id": "037EBB668913D5CC883C", "name": "Ana Melisa Cortz - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "p-YJWdxV8amH5ar5wlvzJA", "camera": {"lon": -86.72398216911445, "lat": 36.07510500813274, "alt": 159.470321654614, "heading": 129.3464467010097, "tilt": 87.01196539832274, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -86.72398216911445, "lat": 36.07510500813274, "for": "Ana Melisa Cortez", "label": "Ana Melisa Cortez - Street View", "still": "86_ana_melisa_cortez_street.jpg"}, {"index": 101, "id": "0E6924E88A13CF576C20", "name": "Joe-Hal Faughn", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Winter Park, Florida, Murdered, October 18, 2000", "html": "<p>Winter Park, Florida, Murdered, October 18, 2000 </p><p>Joe-Hal Faughn was an interior designer who made chandeliers from gold, silver, bronze, and crystal. His artful designs could be found in museum exhibits and houses all across Florida. Joe-Hal hired Frank Knight, a stranger, to drive a few chandeliers to Chicago for a client. A few days later, Joe-Hal’s body was found, choked to death and tied up. Nothing was stolen from his apartment, but his car was missing. It was found a few days later at a nearby church. Frank was a suspect from the beginning but took two years to locate. Investigators traced him to New Orleans and a tip led them to Frank, who was already in prison under an alias on a different attempted-murder charge. </p><p>I made this photograph outside Joe-Hal’s home where he was found dead. </p>", "lon": -81.34792999999999, "lat": 28.5978833, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -81.34792999999999, "lat": 28.5978833, "range": 914.0774214725267, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/joe-hal_faughn_mvc-012s.jpg", "still": "87_joe_hal_faughn_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 102, "id": "0EDEEFC07013D5CDC2E2", "name": "Joe-Hal Faughn - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "Z-gkmuSfjluhVXzAaEENQQ", "camera": {"lon": -81.34795838517105, "lat": 28.59786348346544, "alt": 31.14952023439233, "heading": 347.2887754202909, "tilt": 94.38010016336148, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -81.34795838517105, "lat": 28.59786348346544, "for": "Joe-Hal Faughn", "label": "Joe-Hal Faughn - Street View", "still": "88_joe_hal_faughn_street.jpg"}, {"index": 103, "id": "0E9C86737B13CB2725F5", "name": "Billy Jean Lavette", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "Savannah, Georgia, Murdered, November 20, 2000", "html": "<p>Savannah, Georgia, Murdered, November 20, 2000 </p><p>Billy Jean Lavette was a cosmetologist who lived in Savannah. Billy Jean’s body—clad in women’s clothes, face up, and with a wound in the back of the head—was found by workers laying cable in a wooded area in West Chatham County. The press stuck to the line that Billy Jean “had a criminal history that included four charges of solicitation of sodomy. In his arrest photo, he was shown wearing lipstick and eyeliner.”</p><p>Ross Lane Emerson emerged as a suspect in 2001, when he was going to trial for a murder in Florida. Ross committed suicide in jail.</p><p>I made this photograph close to the location where Billy Jean's body was found.</p>", "lon": -81.21701999999999, "lat": 32.1675617, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -81.21701999999999, "lat": 32.1675617, "range": 850, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/billy_jean_lavette_mavica099.jpg", "still": "89_billy_jean_lavette_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 104, "id": "0579AFB4E113D5D07709", "name": "Billy Jean Lavette - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "dXeU-Q-0QzSCyXwzjS6Wfg", "camera": {"lon": -81.2170561308306, "lat": 32.16751393935733, "alt": 17.34187574448023, "heading": 49.65138164777333, "tilt": 87.37696038067631, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -81.2170561308306, "lat": 32.16751393935733, "for": "Billy Jean Lavette", "label": "Billy Jean Lavette - Street View", "still": "90_billy_jean_lavette_street.jpg"}, {"index": 105, "id": "022CF6D77013CF90803C", "name": "Mark Blum", "kind": "story", "subtitle": "North Miami Beach, Florida, Murdered, August 19, 2000", "html": "<p>North Miami Beach, Florida, Murdered, August 19, 2000 </p><p>Mark Blum was a 48-year-old North Miami Beach High School teacher. He met Allen Blohm, a 20-year-old from Miramar, in an online chat room. The two men dated for three months. On the night of the murder, Mark and Allen got into an argument about their physical relationship. Allen picked up a 15-pound dumbbell and repeatedly struck Mark on the head until he died. Allen then stole Mark’s 1971 Corvette Stingray and fled.</p><p>The Corvette was eventually recovered, and Allen was tracked down. In conversation with police, he revealed details that only Mark’s killer would know and was arrested.</p><p>I made this photograph in approximately the area where Mark lived in North Miami.</p>", "lon": -80.17931329999999, "lat": 25.8488783, "n_images": 1, "lookat": {"lon": -80.17931329999999, "lat": 25.8488783, "range": 939.4273473466238, "heading": 0, "tilt": 0}, "photo": "photos/mark_blum_mavica860.jpg", "still": "91_mark_blum_earth.jpg"}, {"index": 106, "id": "03CED5B9CB13D5D1BB33", "name": "Mark Blum - Map View", "kind": "street", "pano_id": "IbZ-2EqZDnvSkk2KJoLzkA", "camera": {"lon": -80.17919008908606, "lat": 25.84883266391196, "alt": 5.707125186920166, "heading": 273.6730289588116, "tilt": 88.31918706311447, "fov": 59.99999999999999}, "lon": -80.17919008908606, "lat": 25.84883266391196, "for": "Mark Blum", "label": "Mark Blum - Street View", "still": "92_mark_blum_street.jpg"}]};
